465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,26313,979,400,74,87,61,19,10,24,87,61,18,45455,87,61,17,10,18,17,87,12,16,16,3,374,17779,553,400,87,488,220,20,3,374,400,19,12947,220,7379,400,74,2418,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe remainder when $kx^4+9x^3+kx^2+32x-11$ is divided by $x + 5$ is $4$.  Find $k.$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"ea7a3fac64cc9e5bfe1d0b9228ff08748db87a8592f899a8ed6b78bdc252efb5"},"seconds":29.75600242614746,"seed":326481407098253687,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the value of \\( k \\) such that the remainder when the polynomial \\( P(x) = kx^4 + 9x^3 + kx^2 + 32x - 11 \\) is divided by \\( x + 5 \\) is 4, we can use the Remainder Theorem. According to the Remainder Theorem, the remainder of the division of \\( P(x) \\) by \\( x + 5 \\) is equal to \\( P(-5) \\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\nP(-5) = 4\n\\]\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = -5 \\) into the polynomial \\( P(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nP(-5) = k(-5)^4 + 9(-5)^3 + k(-5)^2 + 32(-5) - 11\n\\]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n\\[\n(-5)^4 = 625 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad k(-5)^4 = 625k\n\\]\n\\[\n(-5)^3 = -125 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 9(-5)^3 = 9(-125) = -1125\n\\]\n\\[\n(-5)^2 = 25 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad k(-5)^2 = 25k\n\\]\n\\[\n32(-5) = -160\n\\]\n\\[\n-11 \\quad \\text{remains the same}\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\\[\nP(-5) = 625k - 1125 + 25k - 160 - 11\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\nP(-5) = 650k - 1296\n\\]\n\nSet this equal to the remainder 4:\n\n\\[\n650k - 1296 = 4\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\n650k = 1300\n\\]\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{1300}{650} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( k \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the value of \\( k \\) such that the remainder when the polynomial \\( P(x) = kx^4 + 9x^3 + kx^2 + 32x - 11 \\) is divided by \\( x + 5 \\) is 4, we can use the Remainder Theorem. According to the Remainder Theorem, the remainder of the division of \\( P(x) \\) by \\( x + 5 \\) is equal to \\( P(-5) \\). Therefore, we set up the equation:\n\n\\[\nP(-5) = 4\n\\]\n\nFirst, substitute \\( x = -5 \\) into the polynomial \\( P(x) \\):\n\n\\[\nP(-5) = k(-5)^4 + 9(-5)^3 + k(-5)^2 + 32(-5) - 11\n\\]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\n\\[\n(-5)^4 = 625 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad k(-5)^4 = 625k\n\\]\n\\[\n(-5)^3 = -125 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad 9(-5)^3 = 9(-125) = -1125\n\\]\n\\[\n(-5)^2 = 25 \\quad \\text{so} \\quad k(-5)^2 = 25k\n\\]\n\\[\n32(-5) = -160\n\\]\n\\[\n-11 \\quad \\text{remains the same}\n\\]\n\nPutting it all together:\n\n\\[\nP(-5) = 625k - 1125 + 25k - 160 - 11\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n\\[\nP(-5) = 650k - 1296\n\\]\n\nSet this equal to the remainder 4:\n\n\\[\n650k - 1296 = 4\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( k \\):\n\n\\[\n650k = 1300\n\\]\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{1300}{650} = 2\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( k \\) is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:583"}
